To terminate a C program, you can use the `exit` function from the standard library `<stdlib.h>`. This function immediately ends the execution of the program and returns an integer value to the operating system. Here's how you can use it:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    // Your code here...

    // Terminate the program
    exit(0); // Use 0 to indicate successful termination
    // or
    // exit(1); // Use 1 or any non-zero value to indicate failure

    // The code below this line will not be executed
    return 0; // This is typically unreachable due to the exit call above
}
```

Alternatively, in some cases where you want to clean up resources before exiting, you might use `exit` within a cleanup function that gets called when the program is about to terminate:

